<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jahangir Mirza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1356–1376</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a> Mirza was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> and his intended heir. In the 1370s he was married to Khanzada, a princess of the Sufid dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a>, as part of Timur's dealings with that state. He died young in 1376, and his sons Muhammad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> and Pir Muhammad later figured in Timur's plans for the succession.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jahangir-mirza.jpg" alt="Jahangir Mirza" width="320" height="484" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ẓahīr ud-Dīn Muḥammad Bābur (1483-1530) It contains 30 mostly full-page miniatures in fine Mughal style by at least t... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Babur_being_entertained_in_Ghazn%C4%AB_by_Jah%C4%81ng%C4%ABr_M%C4%ABrz%C4%81.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
